## Native OS widget backends — one class per OS, same method names, so the L2
## framework selects a backend by OS (the device OS by default). Shipped WITH
## canvas (L1, the platform/FFI layer): the per-OS shim (`runtime/cocoa.m`, …) and
## the system frameworks it needs (`[system_libs] frameworks`) ride in-tree, so a
## native build wires everything up out of the box — nothing to install (AppKit,
## Win32, GTK are part of their OS).
##
## Today: macOS (AppKit). Windows (Win32) and Linux (GTK) classes land the same
## way — a sibling `runtime/<os>.c|m` + a class with the SAME method names — so
## quiver's `run_native` only picks `MacOS` / `Windows` / `Linux` by OS.
##
## Off the native OS (e.g. wasm) the `.m` is never compiled and these become
## undefined host imports a web app simply never calls.

## macOS / AppKit backend. Each method is a flat C-ABI call into `runtime/cocoa.m`
## (the `as "cocoa_*"` mints the symbol). The Cocoa framework is declared in
## `Ruxen.toml [system_libs] frameworks` and links automatically on macOS.
##
## Lifecycle: `window_open` → quiver mounts widgets (`create`/`set_*`/`add_subview`)
## → `show` → loop on `pump` (one event per call; returns the node id of a control
## that fired, -1 for nothing, -2 when the window closed).
class MacOS
  lib "runtime/cocoa.m"
    def self.window_open as "cocoa_window_open"(title: &String, w: Int, h: Int) -> nil
    def self.create as "cocoa_create"(id: Int, kind: Int) -> nil
    def self.set_text as "cocoa_set_text"(id: Int, text: &String) -> nil
    def self.set_frame as "cocoa_set_frame"(id: Int, x: Int, y: Int, w: Int, h: Int) -> nil
    def self.set_state as "cocoa_set_state"(id: Int, value: Int) -> nil
    def self.add_subview as "cocoa_add_subview"(parent: Int, child: Int) -> nil
    def self.show as "cocoa_show" -> nil
    def self.pump as "cocoa_pump" -> Int
  end
end
